rules_version = '2';
service cloud.firestore {
  match /databases/{database}/documents {
    function isSignedIn() { return request.auth != null; }
    // Staff access is a Firebase custom claim set via
    // tools/scripts/set-staff-claim.mjs (Aglyn Admin Console, AGL-42).
    //
    // Read with `.get()` — the `staffRole()` idiom below — and NOT as
    // `request.auth.token.staff`, which is an ERROR for the 99.99% of sessions
    // that carry no such claim rather than a `false` (AGL-2479). The outcome
    // never differed: `isStaff()` is only ever the first disjunct of an `||` or
    // the left of an `&&`, and CEL absorbs an error against a `true` and
    // propagates it as a deny against a `false` — exactly what a `false` does.
    // There is no `!isStaff()` anywhere in this file, which is the one shape
    // that WOULD have differed.
    //
    // The MESSAGE differed, and that is the whole reason this changed. Every
    // ordinary denial in the emulator read `Property staff is undefined on
    // object for 'create' @ L<the allow line>`, so the reported cause of a
    // refusal was a red herring pointing at this function, on every rule, for
    // every non-staff user. Three stale tests were misfiled as a rules bug on
    // exactly that evidence. Denials now name the line that actually refused.
    function isStaff() {
      return isSignedIn() && request.auth.token.get('staff', false) == true;
    }
    // Staff RBAC (AGL-206): roles ride a second claim. A missing role means
    // 'support', the LEAST privileged (AGL-2131) — it meant 'super' here
    // long after AGL-495 inverted that default in every API route, so a
    // claim-less staff token was super at the rules layer and support at the
    // handlers. Role-less accounts are found with
    // tools/scripts/audit-staff-claims.mjs and given an explicit role with
    // tools/scripts/set-staff-claim.mjs; they are not admitted by a default.
    // support = read-only surfaces; billing = plan/entitlement writes;
    // super = everything including suspension and user management.
    function staffRole() {
      return request.auth.token.get('staffRole', 'support');
    }
    function isBillingStaff() {
      return isStaff() && (staffRole() == 'super' || staffRole() == 'billing');
    }
    function isSuperStaff() { return isStaff() && staffRole() == 'super'; }

    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    // Media objects (AGL-1881): the fields that address the SHARED Storage
    // bucket, decide who may read what it holds, and decide what comes back.
    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    /**
     * A media document's server-written fields are server-owned facts about a
     * Storage OBJECT. None is user input, and all of them were client-
     * writable. `storagePath` and `private` are the pair the pre-launch
     * review named CRITICAL; the rest are below.
     *
     * `storagePath` is the object key. Seven server paths read it off the
     * document and hand it to `bucket.file(...)` on the ADMIN SDK, which
     * `cloud/firebase-storage.rules` does not govern at all — Admin
     * credentials are not subject to Storage rules. So the field addressed
     * ANY object in the shared bucket, from a document the writer owns:
     * `serveMediaCdn` streams it to an anonymous caller behind an
     * edge-cached URL, `/api/media/replace` overwrites it, and the folder
     * delete removes it. Cross-tenant read, overwrite and destroy. The
     * bucket is shared and also holds the `adminAudit-archive/` and
     * `erasures/` retention prefixes, which are FIXED and guessable rather
     * than needing a stolen key.
     *
     * `private` is the other half of the same hole: `serveMediaCdn` skips
     * the download-signature gate whenever `private != true`, so flipping
     * it made a signed-only asset anonymously fetchable.
     *
     * The SINK is fixed too, and first — `media-storage-path.ts` refuses a
     * key outside the document's own `<scope>/media/` prefix. This is the
     * second layer, and it is not redundant: staff writes bypass the sink's
     * scope by design, rules are deployed on a separate cadence from code,
     * and a sink-only fix is one refactor away from being reintroduced.
     * The place a CLIENT-supplied key can be refused is where the client
     * writes it, which is here.
     *
     * ## Why the list is longer than the two fields the finding named
     *
     * Because the question the finding asked has an answer, and the answer is
     * a SET. `media-write-deny-coverage.spec.ts` derives it: every field the
     * three Admin-SDK creators write onto a media document, minus every field
     * a client surface actually writes. Nothing here is a guess, and nothing
     * here is client-authored — the DAM writes `fileName`, `alt`,
     * `description`, `tags`, `folderId`/`folder` and `visibleTo`, and those
     * six are the whole of it, swept from the repo rather than remembered.
     *
     * Two of the eleven beyond `storagePath`/`private` are security inputs in
     * their own right, found while classifying them and closed here rather
     * than written down as safe:
     *
     *  - `contentSha256` is the QUARANTINE key. `serveMediaCdn` reads it,
     *    asks `mediaCdnServeBlock`, and returns 410 on a hit — so a client
     *    that could rewrite it walked its own asset out of a takedown.
     *  - `contentType` is the served `Content-Type` and decides the response
     *    CSP (`mediaCdnContentSecurityPolicy`). GCS metadata wins when it is
     *    present, so this is a fallback rather than the only input, which is
     *    what makes it a hardening rather than a second CRITICAL.
     *
     * The rest — `cdnPath`, `contentHash`, `createdAt`, `sizeBytes`,
     * `svgSanitized`, `uploadedBy`, `url`, `variants`, `variantsError` — are
     * lower stakes individually, and are frozen for the AGL-1367 reason:
     * every writer is server-side, so denial costs the product nothing, and a
     * per-field argument about which server-written field is harmless is the
     * argument that let four org fields drift in AGL-1354.
     *
     * ## Two functions, not one
     *
     * `resource` does not exist on a create, and `diff()` against a
     * non-existent document is an ERROR — which denies, but for the wrong
     * reason and at the wrong line. On a create the fields must be ABSENT
     * (every legitimate creator is an Admin-SDK route); on an update they
     * must be UNCHANGED, so a patch that spells out a value it already holds
     * still passes — the same AGL-1795/1813/1824 property `diff()` gives
     * everywhere else in this file.
     *
     * The two lists are kept adjacent and identical on purpose, since rules
     * have no constants; `media-write-deny-coverage.spec.ts` asserts they
     * agree, and that both media blocks use them.
     */
    function mediaObjectFieldsUnset() {
      return !request.resource.data.keys().hasAny([
        'storagePath', 'private', 'cdnPath', 'url', 'contentType',
        'contentHash', 'contentSha256', 'sizeBytes', 'variants',
        'variantsError', 'svgSanitized', 'uploadedBy', 'createdAt']);
    }
    function mediaObjectFieldsUnchanged() {
      return !request.resource.data.diff(resource.data).affectedKeys().hasAny([
        'storagePath', 'private', 'cdnPath', 'url', 'contentType',
        'contentHash', 'contentSha256', 'sizeBytes', 'variants',
        'variantsError', 'svgSanitized', 'uploadedBy', 'createdAt']);
    }

    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    // Organizations (AGL-233/235, docs/MULTI_TENANT_FIRESTORE.md): the
    // org is the tenant boundary. Authorization resolves from the single
    // membership doc read below — identical get()s within one evaluation
    // are billed once, so the helpers can share it freely.
    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    match /orgs/{orgId} {
      function isOrgMember() {
        return isSignedIn() &&
          exists(/databases/$(database)/documents/orgs/$(orgId)/members/$(request.auth.uid));
      }
      function orgMember() {
        return get(/databases/$(database)/documents/orgs/$(orgId)/members/$(request.auth.uid)).data;
      }
      function canManageOrg() {
        return isOrgMember() && orgMember().role in ['owner', 'admin'];
      }
      /**
       * A member whose reach is the WHOLE org, not a named set of sites
       * (AGL-1026).
       *
       * Adding a site collaborator writes a real org membership — that is
       * what projects into `memberRoles` and lets them into the console —
       * scoped with `allHosts: false` and a `hostAccess` map naming their
       * sites. `isOrgMember()` is a bare exists() and never looked at that
       * scoping, so a contractor invited to one microsite could read the
       * whole org's CRM, every shared dataset row, the billing state and the
       * full team roster with everyone's email. The console never showed
       * them any of it; the rules are the boundary, and they were open.
       *
       * Owners and admins span the org by definition. Everyone else needs
       * `allHosts` — the same predicate `hostRoleFor` applies in code.
       */
      function isOrgWideMember() {
        return isOrgMember() &&
          (orgMember().role in ['owner', 'admin'] ||
           orgMember().get('allHosts', false) == true);
      }
      function orgNotSuspended() {
        return orgMember().get('orgSuspended', false) != true;
      }
      /**
       * A PER-MEMBER permission override, read straight off the membership
       * doc (AGL-243).
       *
       * The rules gate on ROLE; the page and the API gate on PERMISSION. An
       * admin whose `billing.view` is revoked is correctly refused by both of
       * those — and Firestore still handed the subscription document to their
       * browser. Nothing painted it; it sat in devtools with the Stripe
       * customer id, the price id and the seat add-ons in it.
       *
       * `permissions` is a flat `Record<string, boolean>` on the member doc
       * (`AglynOrgMember`), and `orgMember()` is a get() every caller here
       * already pays for — identical get()s in one evaluation are billed once
       * — so this costs no extra document access.
       *
       * DEFAULT TRUE, and that is the whole safety argument: an absent key
       * means "no override", which is the state of every member in every org
       * today. A default of false would deny every paying owner the moment
       * this ruleset deploys. This narrows the rule for members who carry an
       * explicit `false` and changes nothing for anyone else.
       *
       * ⚠️ THIS IS THE PER-MEMBER LAYER ONLY. The resolver in
       * `resolveOrgPermissions` has three: per-member overrides beat a custom
       * role (`member.roleId` -> `orgs/{orgId}/roles/{roleId}`), which beats
       * the role default. The custom-role layer is NOT enforced here and
       * deliberately so — see the note on the `billing` match below.
       */
      function memberGrants(permission) {
        return orgMember().get('permissions', {}).get(permission, true) == true;
      }

      // Still ANY member, including a scoped collaborator (AGL-1026): this doc
      // carries `plan` and `entitlements`, which every console surface gates
      // features on, so denying it would lock a collaborator out of the site
      // they were invited to.
      //
      // It no longer carries the commercial keys. `stripeCustomerId`,
      // `subscription` and `seatAddons` moved to `billing/stripe` below, gated
      // on `canManageOrg()` (AGL-1028) — Firestore has no field-level reads, so
      // moving them was the only way to narrow this without locking anyone out.
      // What stays is deliberate: `plan`/`entitlements` because that is the
      // whole reason this doc is readable, `suspendedAt`/`suspendedReason`
      // because the tenant runtime reads suspension on every published page
      // render and moving it would buy no confidentiality (this doc is member-
      // readable regardless), and `billingStatus` because the dunning banner is
      // shown to every member on purpose.
      allow read: if isStaff() || isOrgMember();
      // Org creation/deletion and the slug/owner/billing keys are
      // Admin-SDK-only (org APIs, Stripe webhook, staff tooling), with
      // two staff exceptions mirroring the retired tenants rules
      // (AGL-206/238). After AGL-1517/1795/1813/1824 the two staff branches
      // deny the SAME server-owned keys, with exactly two deliberate
      // differences, both asserted by org-write-deny-coverage.spec.ts:
      // super staff keeps `enabledPlugins` (the AGL-501 carve-out) and
      // `erasureRequestedAt` (the erasure toggle's client batch); billing
      // staff keeps the rename. Members with the admin role may rename the
      // org; nothing else is client-writable.
      allow create, delete: if false;
      // `enabledPlugins` is the runtime plugin switchboard, written ONLY by
      // the install API after entitlement + kill-switch checks (AGL-501) —
      // it must stay out of every client-writable branch, or an admin can
      // enable gated/paid plugins for free.
      //
      // The same reasoning reaches four more keys the list had drifted past
      // (AGL-1354). None of them looks commercial, and all four are the input
      // to a decision made somewhere else:
      //  - `brandingProfile` — what `resolveBrandingProfile` applies behind
      //    the `whiteLabel` entitlement, written only by /api/orgs/settings
      //    `update-branding` after `checkEntitlement(org, 'whiteLabel')`.
      //    White-label is Enterprise/Agency; the console gates it, but the
      //    console is not a boundary, and AGL-1099 turns
      //    `brandingProfile.customConsoleDomain` into a ROUTING input — a
      //    client-writable hostname deciding which org a domain serves;
      //  - `sso` — the GCIP tenant/provider `sso-lookup` and `sso-jit` sign
      //    the org in through, written only by /api/orgs/sso behind the
      //    `ssoEnabled` entitlement. Client-writable, it is an auth-routing
      //    field, not a settings field;
      //  - `discount`/`enterprise` — staff-set commercial markers. They grant
      //    no entitlement, but they are what the billing page and the staff
      //    MRR rollup report, so a self-served value quietly falsifies
      //    revenue.
      // Every console surface writes all four through an Admin-SDK route, so
      // denying them client-side costs the product nothing.
      //
      // `plan`, `entitlements` and `releaseFlags` are Admin-SDK-only for
      // EVERY client, staff included (AGL-1795). AGL-1786 moved the staff
      // override to /api/admin/org-override, which was the last client
      // writer of the three: what remains on this document from a browser is
      // the org rename (`canManageOrg()`, which already excludes them) and
      // the erasure toggle's `erasureRequestedAt`. So denying them costs the
      // product nothing, and it is what makes the reason gate a BOUNDARY —
      // `adminAudit` validates no shape at all (`allow create: if isStaff()`),
      // so while a staff session could still write these keys directly it
      // could change a fee percentage and write a reasonless audit row, or
      // none. AGL-1652 declined to police the reason's shape in these rules,
      // and that judgement stands: validating one action's field here would
      // imply the other client-written rows are validated when they are not.
      // Closing the WRITE is the thing rules can say honestly, so that is
      // what they say.
      //
      // This empties the `isBillingStaff()` branch of its original purpose —
      // plan and entitlement writes were the whole point of that role — and
      // that is the honest outcome rather than a regression: billing staff
      // still make those changes, through the route, where the role is
      // re-checked.
      //
      // THE ROLE SPLIT IS NOT EXPRESSED HERE ANY MORE, and cannot be. It was
      // `releaseFlags` super-only (AGL-1635: the per-org override of "is this
      // feature launched", matching the super-staff-only platform-wide editor
      // at /api/admin/flags, and not a billing act since nothing about it is
      // priced) while `plan`/`entitlements` stayed open to billing staff.
      // Once both branches deny all three keys there is no split left for a
      // rule to draw. It lives in the route, restated server-side because the
      // Admin SDK bypasses rules — and enforced there on the CHANGE, not the
      // payload, exactly as `affectedKeys()` does below: every override write
      // NAMES `releaseFlags`, so refusing billing staff for naming it
      // unchanged would take away quota overrides they can make today.
      //
      // The org `suspended*` family is Admin-SDK-only for EVERY client,
      // super staff included (AGL-1517). AGL-1505 removed the last client
      // writer — the legacy staff toggle now POSTs /api/admin/lockdown — and
      // a bare client write would set the flag without the four effects that
      // make a suspension real (the `orgSuspended` member projection fan-out,
      // token revocation, tenant ISR eviction, the audit row). This mirrors
      // the AGL-1501 host takedown keys below. `erasureRequestedAt` stays
      // super-writable: the erasure toggle still legitimately writes it
      // client-side.
      //
      // `discount`, `enterprise`, `brandingProfile` and `billingStatus` are
      // Admin-SDK-only for EVERY client, staff included (AGL-1813) — the
      // AGL-1354 reasoning that closed them to an org admin reaches a staff
      // browser console just as well, and doing it client-side leaves no
      // `adminAudit` row. Re-established per key, as AGL-1795's method
      // requires:
      //  - `discount` — written only by /api/admin/org-discount (staff
      //    apply/remove, audited) and the Stripe webhook's coupon mirror;
      //  - `enterprise` — the pre-AGL-1118 comped marker. NOTHING in the
      //    product writes it any more; `isEnterpriseOrg` only reads it, and
      //    the one writer anywhere is tools/scripts/migrate-enterprise-plan
      //    .mjs, an audited Admin-SDK migration;
      //  - `brandingProfile` — written only by /api/orgs/settings
      //    `update-branding` behind `checkEntitlement(org, 'whiteLabel')`,
      //    and a ROUTING input via `customConsoleDomain` (AGL-1099);
      //  - `billingStatus` — the derived dunning mirror `writeOrgBilling`
      //    stamps from `subscription.status`; a hand-set value would just be
      //    a lie until the next webhook.
      // The only client Web SDK write to a top-level org document anywhere
      // is still the erasure batch (re-swept for AGL-1813), so denying the
      // four costs the product nothing.
      //
      // `seatAddons`, `stripeCustomerId` and `subscription` — the org-doc
      // copies AGL-1028 moved to `billing/stripe` below — are Admin-SDK-only
      // for EVERY client, staff included (AGL-1824). Vestigial but NOT dead:
      // `readOrgBilling` falls back to the org-doc fields when the billing
      // subdoc is missing, and `findOrgIdByStripeCustomer` falls back to a
      // `where('stripeCustomerId' == …)` query on `orgs` — so a client-written
      // `stripeCustomerId` on an org without a billing doc is an input to
      // webhook → org resolution. Re-established per key (AGL-1795's method):
      //  - all three are written only by `writeOrgBilling` (Admin-SDK batch;
      //    `writeInline` defaults OFF and nothing passes it) and the
      //    backfill-org-billing.mjs / drop-inline-org-billing.mjs scripts;
      //  - both fallbacks are Admin-SDK READS, so denying the client write
      //    cannot break the healing path — it only closes the poisoning one.
      // The identity keys `sso`/`slug`/`ownerUid`/`hosts` join the super
      // branch's list for the reasons that already put them on the other two
      // (AGL-1354/1824): `sso` is the auth-routing field written only by
      // /api/orgs/sso behind the `ssoEnabled` entitlement; `slug` is written
      // only by the organizations.ts create/rename transactions that keep the
      // `orgSlugs` index true; `ownerUid` only by org create and the
      // ownership transfer in /api/orgs/settings; `hosts` only by the host
      // APIs and the erase path. A bare client write of any of them bypasses
      // the index/projection maintenance that makes the value mean anything.
      //
      // `createdByUid` joins that identity set (AGL-2265), and it is the one
      // key here that is never written again after the org exists. It is the
      // creator attribution the free-workspace ceiling counts against —
      // stamped once inside `createOrganization`'s transaction and mutated by
      // nothing, `transferOrgOwnership` included — which is precisely what
      // stops "hand a workspace to an alt account, create a fourth, take it
      // back" from being a way past the ceiling. A client able to write it
      // could clear its own attribution and mint free workspaces without
      // limit, so this deny IS the control rather than paperwork around it.
      //
      // The only client Web SDK write to a top-level org document is still
      // the erasure batch (re-swept for AGL-1824), so none of the seven has a
      // client writer to lose.
      //
      // `storageOverage` joins them too (AGL-1886) and is the sharpest of the
      // set: it is what lets an upload past the plan's `storagePerHostMb`
      // hard cap, AND it carries the monthly spend ceiling that bounds the
      // charge. A client able to write it could grant itself unbounded
      // storage and raise its own spend limit in a single merge. Written only
      // by /api/billing/storage-overage (billing.manage), Admin SDK.
      //
      // `registerAllocations` joins all three lists on the same reasoning
      // (AGL-1775). It is an ENTITLEMENT INPUT — it decides how many POS
      // registers a site may run, on top of the plan cap — and it is written
      // only by /api/billing/register-allocations (billing.manage) and by
      // `eraseHost`, both Admin SDK. A client that could write it would assign
      // itself the whole purchased pool on every site, which is precisely the
      // over-entitlement the pool was introduced to close.
      //
      // `collaboratorAllocations` joins them for the identical reason
      // (AGL-2439) — the same pool mechanism on the `members` key. It decides
      // how many console collaborators a site may hold on top of the plan
      // cap, and is written only by /api/billing/collaborator-allocations
      // (billing.manage) and by `eraseHost`, both Admin SDK. A client that
      // could write it would hand itself the whole purchased pool on every
      // site — the exact over-entitlement AGL-2439 closes.
      allow update: if (isSuperStaff() &&
          !request.resource.data.diff(resource.data).affectedKeys().hasAny(
            ['slug', 'ownerUid', 'createdByUid', 'hosts', 'sso',
             'plan', 'entitlements', 'releaseFlags',
             'seatAddons', 'registerAllocations', 'collaboratorAllocations',
             'storageOverage',
             'bandwidthCap', 'stripeCustomerId', 'subscription',
             'billingStatus', 'brandingProfile', 'discount', 'enterprise',
             'suspendedAt', 'suspendedReason', 'suspendedReasonCode',
             'suspendedMessage', 'suspendedUntilMs', 'suspendedMode',
             'suspendedEnforcement'])) ||
        (isBillingStaff() &&
          !request.resource.data.diff(resource.data).affectedKeys().hasAny(
            ['slug', 'ownerUid', 'createdByUid', 'hosts', 'enabledPlugins',
             'sso',
             'plan', 'entitlements', 'releaseFlags',
             'seatAddons', 'registerAllocations', 'collaboratorAllocations',
             'storageOverage',
             'bandwidthCap', 'stripeCustomerId', 'subscription',
             'billingStatus', 'brandingProfile', 'discount', 'enterprise',
             'suspendedAt', 'suspendedReason', 'suspendedReasonCode',
             'suspendedMessage', 'suspendedUntilMs', 'suspendedMode',
             'suspendedEnforcement',
             'erasureRequestedAt'])) ||
        (canManageOrg() && orgNotSuspended() &&
          !request.resource.data.diff(resource.data).affectedKeys().hasAny(
            ['slug', 'ownerUid', 'createdByUid', 'hosts', 'plan',
             'entitlements', 'releaseFlags',
             'enabledPlugins', 'seatAddons', 'registerAllocations',
             'collaboratorAllocations', 'storageOverage', 'bandwidthCap',
             'stripeCustomerId', 'subscription',
             'billingStatus', 'brandingProfile', 'sso', 'discount',
             'enterprise',
             // AGL-1881. Server-owned, and they were client-writable because
             // the coverage guard could not see them: its universe is
             // `AglynOrgBilling` + this deny-list + the entitlement resolvers
             // in `libs/aglyn/src/lib/app-utils` + the seed writer, and all
             // three of these are written by API ROUTES and declared in none
             // of the four. `apps/console/app/api/billing/usage-budget`'s own
             // docblock asserted "the rules deny to every client", which was
             // simply untrue. The blind spot is the finding; these are the
             // instance of it.
             'usageBudget', 'usageAlerts', 'usageAlertsSeeded',
             'suspendedAt', 'suspendedReason', 'suspendedReasonCode',
             'suspendedMessage', 'suspendedUntilMs', 'suspendedMode',
             'suspendedEnforcement',
             'erasureRequestedAt',
             // AGL-2501. The SEARCH KEYS behind the staff organization list.
             // Derived from `name` by `nameSearchFields` on every writer, so
             // there is no state in which a client should set one — and a
             // client that could would decide which name its organization is
             // FOUND by, independently of the name it displays: a workspace
             // reachable by somebody else's brand, or reachable by nothing at
             // all while looking perfectly normal on the staff list.
             'nameLower', 'nameTokens', 'nameReversed']));

      /**
       * The commercial keys, moved off the org doc so a scoped site
       * collaborator stops reading them (AGL-1028): `stripeCustomerId`,
       * `subscription`, `seatAddons`.
       *
       * Managers and staff only. Written exclusively by the Stripe webhook and
       * the billing/staff APIs through the Admin SDK, which rules never
       * applied to — so `write: false` costs nothing and keeps the client out.
       *
       * The org doc keeps a derived `billingStatus` string for the AGL-275
       * dunning banner, which is shown to every member on purpose. That is the
       * only thing about a subscription a non-manager can still see, and it
       * carries no amount, price id or customer id.
       */
      /**
       * ROLE ALONE WAS NOT THE GATE THE PRODUCT ENFORCES (AGL-243).
       *
       * This read `isStaff() || canManageOrg()`. The page and the API both
       * gate on the PERMISSION `billing.view`, which a per-member override can
       * revoke from an admin — so such a member was refused by the console and
       * by every route, and Firestore delivered this document to their browser
       * anyway. `memberGrants` closes that for the per-member layer at zero
       * extra document reads.
       *
       * `canManageOrg()` is unchanged and still leads: this is a narrowing,
       * not a re-grant. There is exactly ONE match block on this path — the
       * org block deliberately has no `{document=**}` catch-all (see the note
       * further down) — so there is no looser sibling to defeat it.
       *
       * ⛔ STILL OPEN: the CUSTOM-ROLE layer. `member.roleId` points at
       * `orgs/{orgId}/roles/{roleId}`, whose map can also revoke
       * `billing.view`, and rules cannot resolve that faithfully without
       * a second cross-document get() plus a correct three-layer precedence
       * (per-member beats custom role beats role default) reimplemented in
       * CEL. A naive OR of the two layers OVER-denies — an admin whose custom
       * role revokes `billing.view` but whose per-member map re-grants it is
       * permitted by the resolver and would be locked out by the rule — and a
       * dangling `roleId` makes get() return null, which denies where the
       * resolver falls back to the role defaults. Both failure modes lock out
       * paying customers, so it is not being attempted during the freeze.
       *
       * The fix that layer needs is DENORMALISATION, not a cleverer rule:
       * `syncOrgAuthProjections` already writes `scopeTokens` onto the member
       * doc for exactly this reason. Extend it to write the RESOLVED map —
       *
       *   orgs/{orgId}/members/{uid}.resolvedPermissions:
       *     Record<string, boolean>   // all three layers already applied
       *
       * — recomputed whenever the member, their `roleId`, or that role doc
       * changes, and then this rule becomes `memberGrants` against
       * `resolvedPermissions` with the same default-true backfill semantics.
       */
      match /billing/{billingDocId} {
        allow read: if isStaff() ||
          (canManageOrg() && memberGrants('billing.view'));
        allow write: if false;
      }

      // The roster carries names and emails, denormalized for the team page,
      // so it is visible to the org — not to a site collaborator, who has no
      // business knowing who else the workspace employs (AGL-1026). Reading
      // your OWN membership stays open to everyone: it is how the console
      // resolves the reader's role and site access, and refusing it would
      // lock scoped collaborators out of their own sites.
      //
      // Mutations go through the membership API so the reverse index and host
      // projections stay consistent.
      match /members/{memberUid} {
        allow read: if isStaff() ||
          (isSignedIn() && memberUid == request.auth.uid) ||
          isOrgWideMember();
        allow write: if false;
      }

      // Invites carry emails — readable by org managers only; the invited
      // account accepts through the API, never by writing here.
      match /invites/{inviteId} {
        allow read: if isStaff() || canManageOrg();
        allow write: if false;
      }

      // Custom roles (AGL-243): named permission sets members resolve
      // their own access from — member-readable, API-written only.
      match /roles/{roleId} {
        allow read: if isStaff() || isOrgMember();
        allow write: if false;
      }

      // Monthly usage rollups (AGL-41) + the live API-request meter
      // (AGL-635): readable by the org so the Billing page usage meters show
      // real numbers — and org-wide only (AGL-1026), because how much the
      // workspace spends is not a fact about the one site a collaborator was
      // invited to. Written only by the server — the report-usage cron and the
      // customer-API auth chokepoint via the Admin SDK, which bypasses rules.
      match /usage/{month} {
        allow read: if isStaff() || isOrgWideMember();
        allow write: if false;
      }
      match /apiUsage/{month} {
        allow read: if isStaff() || isOrgWideMember();
        allow write: if false;
      }

      // Per-asset media delivery counters for the ORG library (AGL-176),
      // added here because they were never granted at all (AGL-1143).
      //
      // This block is a MISSING rule, not a widened one. `serve-media-cdn.ts`
      // writes `{orgs|hosts}/{scopeId}/analytics/{day}` on every origin serve,
      // and `media-library.component.tsx` reads thirty day-docs each time the
      // asset drawer opens — but only the HOST half ever resolved to a rule,
      // through `hosts/{hostId}/{subcollection}/{document=**}`. The org block
      // has no catch-all, so the org half reached no `match` and was denied by
      // default, for EVERY role including staff: a path that matches nothing
      // never gets as far as evaluating `isStaff()`. Measured on production
      // 2026-08-12 — 75 rejected listen targets on this path and not one
      // successful read, from a `staff: true` session whose other reads in the
      // same minute all succeeded.
      //
      // Org-wide only, like `usage` above and unlike `counters` below. One
      // day-doc holds a `media` map keyed by every asset id in the org, and
      // AGL-1041/1042 exist precisely so a site collaborator sees only the
      // assets in their scope — granting `isOrgMember()` here would hand them
      // the delivery figures, and the existence, of all of it in a single
      // document rules cannot filter by field.
      //
      // Server-written only, for the AGL-1367 reason its host twin is on all
      // three exclusion lists: `analytics/{day}` is a metered-invoice input,
      // so a client that could write it could lower a bill.
      match /analytics/{day} {
        allow read: if isStaff() || isOrgWideMember();
        allow write: if false;
      }

      // Per-plugin settings (AGL-428): overrides for a plugin's declared
      // config schema. Readers defensively coerce types (the doc is
      // manager-writable), so junk here can misconfigure only that
      // plugin, never break it.
      match /pluginSettings/{pluginId} {
        allow read: if isStaff() || isOrgMember();
        allow write: if isStaff() || (canManageOrg() && orgNotSuspended());
      }

      // Org-shared data (AGL-237): datasets (records included), contacts
      // and segments are shared across every host in the org. Any member
      // reads; editors and up write; viewers stay read-only. Suspension
      // rides the member doc like org renames.
      //
      // AGL-1026 split this block. Contacts, segments and lists moved to
      // org-wide readers (they are people, see below). Datasets, media and
      // media folders deliberately did NOT: a site's pages bind org datasets
      // and place org media, so a collaborator building that site has to read
      // them, and there is no per-site association to filter on — a dataset
      // belongs to the org and nothing records which sites use it. Scoping
      // these needs that association to exist first, which is a schema change,
      // not a rules edit.
      //
      // AGL-1037/1038 made that schema change, so the scoping below is now
      // possible: resources carry `visibleTo` (['org'] or ['host:{id}', …])
      // and members carry a denormalized `scopeTokens`. The intersection is
      // the association that was missing.
      function canWriteOrgData() {
        return isOrgMember() &&
          orgMember().role in ['owner', 'admin', 'editor'] &&
          orgNotSuspended();
      }
      /**
       * Write access to the org-wide records a site collaborator cannot read
       * (AGL-1026). Without this, an org role of `editor` scoped to one site
       * could still WRITE the contacts and lists the read rules now deny
       * them — write-only access to other people's customer data, which is a
       * stranger position than either allowing or denying both.
       */
      function canWriteOrgWideData() {
        return isOrgWideMember() &&
          orgMember().role in ['owner', 'admin', 'editor'] &&
          orgNotSuspended();
      }
      /**
       * Scoped-resource read (AGL-1041/1042): may the caller see THIS doc?
       *
       * Org-wide members read everything the org owns — they are the org.
       * Everyone else needs their `scopeTokens` (AGL-1038, written by
       * `syncOrgAuthProjections`) to intersect the resource's `visibleTo`.
       * Rules have no `.map()`, which is why both sides are denormalized
       * arrays: `hasAny` is the only set-intersection primitive available.
       *
       * NO missing-field escape hatch, deliberately (AGL-1047). An earlier
       * version allowed a doc with no `visibleTo` through, as safety for the
       * window between the AGL-1040 backfill and this rule deploying. That
       * `in` check made the whole rule unprovable per-document for LIST
       * queries, and Firestore then admitted an UNFILTERED list of the
       * entire collection — including other sites' restricted docs. Verified
       * against the emulator: with the hatch a scoped member lists
       * everything; without it the unfiltered list is denied and the
       * filtered one succeeds.
       *
       * The hatch is also no longer needed: the backfill has run in
       * production (zero docs left unstamped) and every writer now stamps
       * the field, so an absent `visibleTo` means a bug, and failing closed
       * on it is correct.
       */
      function canReadScoped() {
        return isOrgWideMember() ||
          (isOrgMember() &&
           resource.data.visibleTo.hasAny(orgMember().get('scopeTokens', ['org'])));
      }
      function canWriteScopedOrgData() {
        return canWriteOrgData() && canReadScoped();
      }
      /**
       * Only an org-wide member may change who a resource is shared with.
       * Without this a scoped editor could widen their own site's dataset to
       * `['org']`, or — worse — narrow an org-wide one to just their site,
       * taking it away from everyone else.
       */
      function scopeUnchanged() {
        return !request.resource.data.diff(resource.data)
          .affectedKeys().hasAny(['visibleTo']);
      }
      /**
       * Create-time twin of `canReadScoped` — it reads `request.resource`
       * because `resource` does not exist yet. Stops a scoped member
       * creating a resource they would immediately be unable to see, or
       * quietly seeding one into another site's scope.
       */
      function canCreateScoped() {
        return isOrgWideMember() ||
          request.resource.data.visibleTo.hasAny(orgMember().get('scopeTokens', ['org']));
      }
      // Dataset/record CREATES are API-only (AGL-473): the console route
      // enforces the dataStore entitlement and dataset/record quotas
      // server-side. Record updates/deletes stay editor-writable — they
      // don't consume quota.
      // Deleting the DATASET doc is API-only too (AGL-945): the doc owns a
      // `records` subcollection and Firestore doesn't cascade, so a client
      // delete orphaned every record beneath it (still billed, still
      // matched by the rule below, which doesn't check that its parent
      // exists). /api/resources/erase recursiveDeletes the whole subtree.
      // Scoped to the sites that may see them (AGL-1041). NOTE for anyone
      // debugging "the Data page is empty for a collaborator": rules
      // evaluate per document on a LIST, and Firestore rejects the whole
      // query if any candidate would fail — so a scoped member MUST send
      // `where('visibleTo', 'array-contains-any', scopeTokens)`. An
      // unfiltered list is permission-denied, not a filtered result. That
      // is the enforcement working; the client side is AGL-1044.
      match /datasets/{datasetId} {
        allow read: if isStaff() || canReadScoped();
        allow create: if isStaff();
        allow update: if isStaff() ||
          (canWriteScopedOrgData() && (isOrgWideMember() || scopeUnchanged()));
        allow delete: if isStaff();
        match /records/{recordId} {
          // A record has no `visibleTo` of its own; it inherits the
          // dataset's. That costs one extra document read per evaluation,
          // including per candidate on a list query. The alternative —
          // denormalizing `visibleTo` onto every record — is cheaper to
          // read and much harder to keep correct when a dataset's scope
          // changes (an N-record backfill on every scope edit, with a
          // window where records disagree with their parent). Correctness
          // wins here; revisit if record listing shows up in Firestore
          // costs.
          function parentDatasetVisible() {
            return isOrgWideMember() ||
              (isOrgMember() &&
               get(/databases/$(database)/documents/orgs/$(orgId)/datasets/$(datasetId))
                 .data.visibleTo
                 .hasAny(orgMember().get('scopeTokens', ['org'])));
          }
          allow read: if isStaff() || parentDatasetVisible();
          allow create: if isStaff();
          allow update, delete: if isStaff() ||
            (canWriteOrgData() && parentDatasetVisible());
        }
      }
      // The CRM is org-wide-only (AGL-1026). Contacts and segments are people
      // — names, emails, everything a form ever collected, across every site
      // — and there is no per-site slice of them to hand a collaborator: a
      // contact belongs to the org, not to the site whose form created it.
      // Given the choice between showing a one-site contractor the whole
      // customer list and showing them none of it, none is the only defensible
      // one. A collaborator who genuinely needs their site's leads should get
      // them through a server route that filters, not a widened rule.
      match /contacts/{contactId} {
        allow read: if isStaff() || isOrgWideMember();
        allow write: if isStaff() || canWriteOrgWideData();
      }
      match /contactSegments/{segmentId} {
        allow read: if isStaff() || isOrgWideMember();
        allow write: if isStaff() || canWriteOrgWideData();
      }

      // Email topics: the streams a recipient may leave one at a time.
      // Scoped like `lists` and for the same reason — a topic is authored
      // editorial content an agency writes once and points several sites at,
      // so the definitions are org-shared while a recipient's OPT-OUT is
      // per site, at `hosts/{hostId}/topicOptOuts`.
      //
      // Read is org-wide like the CRM it sits beside, and NOT public: the
      // catalog names an org's internal mail streams. The unauthenticated
      // preference page reads it on the Admin SDK behind a verified HMAC,
      // which is a different question from whether a browser may.
      //
      // Delete is refused outright, matching the card, and that is the rule
      // rather than a convenience. A topic id is signed into every
      // unsubscribe link sent under it, and those links live in inboxes
      // indefinitely — a deleted topic is a preference page that cannot name
      // the message the recipient is holding. Retiring writes
      // `archived: true`, which is an update.
      match /emailTopics/{topicId} {
        allow read: if isStaff() || isOrgWideMember();
        allow create, update: if isStaff() || canWriteOrgWideData();
        allow delete: if isStaff();
      }

      // Email lists (AGL-254): static audiences shared across the org's
      // hosts; the enrollList automation step writes members server-side.
      // Deleting the LIST doc is API-only (AGL-946): its `members` are
      // enrolled contacts (PII) in a subcollection Firestore won't cascade
      // into, so a client delete left them readable to every org member
      // with no list to reach them from. /api/resources/erase walks the
      // whole subtree; enrollments below stay individually deletable.
      match /lists/{listId} {
        // Enrolled members are contacts, so the list follows the CRM
        // (AGL-1026): org-wide readers only.
        allow read: if isStaff() || isOrgWideMember();
        allow create, update: if isStaff() || canWriteOrgWideData();
        allow delete: if isStaff();
        // ENROLLING is API-only; REMOVING is not, and the asymmetry is the
        // point. A member document carries the consent basis that says why
        // this person may be mailed — `marketingConsent`, its timestamp, the
        // basis and the account answerable for it. A client that can write
        // one can write `marketingConsent: true` beside an address nothing
        // has checked, which is a consent record minted by pressing a button:
        // no stored opt-in consulted, no suppression list consulted, and no
        // way afterwards to tell it from one somebody actually gave. So the
        // create and the update belong to the route
        // (`plugins-email/server-console.ts` and the Inbox's assign-list),
        // which runs the shared `list-assignment-policy` and writes through
        // `enrollListMember`.
        //
        // `write` covered all three until AGL-254's console surface gained an
        // add button; nothing had ever used the client create, so closing it
        // takes nothing away. Removal stays a client delete because taking
        // somebody OFF a list needs no basis and destroys no evidence anybody
        // relies on — the refusal it must never be confused with lives on the
        // suppression list, which this block does not touch.
        match /members/{memberId} {
          allow read: if isStaff() || isOrgWideMember();
          allow create, update: if isStaff();
          allow delete: if isStaff() || canWriteOrgWideData();
        }
      }

      // Org media library (AGL-237, full DAM parity): shareable with any
      // host in the org. Storage objects stay API-managed (upload/delete
      // routes enforce quota), but editors mutate the metadata docs the
      // same way they do on a host's library (rename, folder moves).
      //
      // Scoped like datasets (AGL-1042). Assets carry their OWN `visibleTo`
      // rather than inheriting their folder's: the tree is five levels deep
      // (`media-folders.ts`), so read-time inheritance would cost up to five
      // `get()`s per evaluation. A folder's scope is instead applied to its
      // contents by an explicit, auditable write-time cascade in the console
      // (AGL-1045) — and moving an asset between folders does NOT re-scope
      // it, which the UI has to say out loud because the opposite is what
      // people assume.
      // `write` is split into create/update/delete here because
      // `canReadScoped()` inspects `resource.data`, which does not exist on
      // a create — media folders ARE created client-side, so that path is
      // real and would error rather than deny.
      // The object fields are frozen on both operations (AGL-1881). This block
      // used to freeze `visibleTo` and nothing else, so an org editor could
      // point their own asset at any object key in the shared bucket — see
      // `mediaObjectFieldsUnset` at the top of this file for what that bought
      // and for the whole list. Nothing legitimate is lost: every writer of
      // every frozen field is an Admin-SDK route, and the DAM's client writes
      // are `fileName`, `alt`, `description`, `tags`, `folderId`/`folder` and
      // `visibleTo`.
      match /media/{mediaId} {
        allow read: if isStaff() || canReadScoped();
        allow create: if isStaff() ||
          (canWriteOrgData() && canCreateScoped() && mediaObjectFieldsUnset());
        allow update: if isStaff() ||
          (canWriteScopedOrgData() && (isOrgWideMember() || scopeUnchanged()) &&
           mediaObjectFieldsUnchanged());
        allow delete: if isStaff() || canWriteScopedOrgData();
      }
      // Folders hold a name and a parent; they address no Storage object, so
      // the object freeze has nothing to say here and is deliberately absent
      // rather than copied for symmetry.
      match /mediaFolders/{folderId} {
        allow read: if isStaff() || canReadScoped();
        allow create: if isStaff() || (canWriteOrgData() && canCreateScoped());
        allow update: if isStaff() ||
          (canWriteScopedOrgData() && (isOrgWideMember() || scopeUnchanged()));
        allow delete: if isStaff() || canWriteScopedOrgData();
      }
      // Storage quota meter for the org library.
      match /counters/{counterId} {
        allow read: if isStaff() || isOrgMember();
        allow write: if false;
      }

      // Org-tier plugin installs (AGL-237): pins that apply to every host
      // in the org; written only by the install API (entitlement + kill
      // switch checks live there).
      match /installs/{listingId} {
        allow read: if isStaff() || isOrgMember();
        allow write: if false;
      }

      // Org activity log: mirrors hosts/{hostId}/activity. It names who did
      // what across every site, which is both a roster leak and a map of work
      // on sites the reader cannot see (AGL-1026). A collaborator still gets
      // their own sites' log at `hosts/{hostId}/activity`.
      //
      // NO CUSTOMER READS IT FROM A CLIENT (AGL-2444). This was
      // `isOrgWideMember()`, which is the ROSTER question and knows nothing
      // about the granular permission catalog — so `org.auditLog` was a
      // display gate on the team page and nothing else: a member whose custom
      // role revoked it read the feed straight out of any Firestore client.
      // `/api/orgs/activity` is now the only door, and it checks the
      // permission with the Admin SDK, which bypasses these rules. Denying
      // here is therefore what makes that check the access control rather
      // than a second opinion.
      //
      // Staff keep the direct read: the staff console reads across orgs by
      // design and is gated by the `staff` claim, not by a customer's role.
      match /activity/{entryId} {
        allow read: if isStaff();
        allow write: if false;
      }

      /**
       * The §512(i) repeat-infringer strike ledger (AGL-1983). One document
       * per upheld copyright notice, keyed by the abuse report's id.
       *
       * There is no catch-all under `orgs/{orgId}` — every subcollection here
       * is matched explicitly — so this collection was already deny-all by
       * the absence of a rule. It is written out anyway, because "safe
       * because nobody wrote a rule" is a property that evaporates the day
       * somebody adds a convenience wildcard, and this is the ledger that
       * decides whether an account gets terminated.
       *
       * STAFF-READ, and deliberately NOT readable by the org it counts. That
       * is not secrecy for its own sake: §512(i) also requires that
       * subscribers be INFORMED of the policy, and they are — by the
       * published policy, and by the notice staff send on each strike. What
       * the raw ledger adds is the complainants' identities and the exact
       * count, and an org that could watch its own count in real time could
       * time its behaviour against the threshold, which is the one reading
       * that would make the policy easier rather than harder to game.
       *
       * NOBODY-WRITE. A client able to write here could delete its own
       * strikes, which is the account-termination condition of the entire
       * safe harbour. Written only by /api/admin/abuse-reports, Admin SDK,
       * beside its `adminAudit` row.
       */
      match /dmcaStrikes/{reportId} {
        allow read: if isStaff();
        allow write: if false;
      }

      // SSO domain claims (AGL-1210). Deny ALL client access, read included.
      //
      // The claim token is the only thing standing between an org and a
      // domain it does not own: publish that token as a TXT record and the
      // domain's sign-ins route to your IdP. A client-readable claim would let
      // any org member — or anyone who can read the collection — lift another
      // org's token, so the console goes through /api/orgs/sso, which returns
      // a claim only to an admin of THAT org.
      //
      // Writes are API-only for the stronger reason: `verified` is the whole
      // security property, and a client that can set it has defeated domain
      // verification entirely.
      //
      // AGL-1887 added a SECOND way to satisfy that gate, which makes this
      // block load-bearing in a new way. `publishSsoDomains` now publishes on
      // `verified == true` OR a non-empty string `attestedBy` — a named staff
      // member vouching for an org onboarded by hand before self-serve, whose
      // domain has no DNS proof behind it and never will. `attestedBy` skips
      // the proof, so a client able to write it could publish routing for a
      // domain it does not own, which is the same takeover the `verified`
      // check exists to stop. Deny-all is what keeps the marker meaningful.
      //
      // STAFF ARE DENIED HERE TOO, and that is not an oversight: the only
      // writer is `attestSsoDomain` (and tools/scripts/attest-sso-domain.mjs)
      // through the Admin SDK, which bypasses rules. A staff browser session
      // has no reason to hold a write that lands beside an `adminAudit` row.
      // `cloud/rules-tests/firestore-rules.test.mjs` is what goes red if any
      // of this is loosened.
      match /ssoDomains/{domain} {
        allow read, write: if false;
      }

      // Custom email sending domains. Admin SDK only, on the same reasoning as
      // `ssoDomains` above and for a closely related security property.
      //
      // `status` is what decides whether mail may leave as this domain. A
      // client that could write it would set `verified` on a domain whose DKIM
      // record was never published — sending as a domain it does not control,
      // which is the whole of what the DNS check exists to prevent. It would
      // also defeat the refusal the send path is built around, since that
      // refusal reads exactly this field.
      //
      // The read denial stands on its own: the document holds `dkimSelector`,
      // and knowing which selector an org was issued is the first half of
      // impersonating its sending setup.
      match /sendingDomains/{domain} {
        allow read, write: if false;
      }
    }

    // Workspace-subdomain resolution ({slug}.aglyn.com): public read so
    // the console can resolve a slug pre-auth; reservation is API-only.
    match /orgSlugs/{slug} {
      allow read: if true;
      allow write: if false;
    }

    // Email domain → SSO identity pool (AGL-1101/1210). Resolved pre-auth by
    // /api/auth/sso-lookup through the Admin SDK, which bypasses rules — so
    // unlike `orgSlugs` this needs no public read, and does not get one.
    //
    // A live doc here is the statement "this org proved it owns this domain",
    // and it is the routing decision for every sign-in on that domain. Written
    // by `publishSsoDomains` alone, which re-checks the DNS-verified claim
    // before each write. Any client write path would be a domain-takeover
    // vector, so there is none.
    match /ssoDomains/{domain} {
      allow read, write: if false;
    }

    // Custom console domain → org (AGL-1099/AGL-1373). Admin SDK only, on the
    // same reasoning as `ssoDomains` and deliberately NOT public-read like
    // `orgSlugs` — nothing client-side consults it, and `orgSlugs` is public
    // only because it doubles as the health probe.
    //
    // A doc here is a RESERVATION as much as a routing record: `status` and
    // `txtToken` are the whole ownership proof, and every name in `names` is
    // claimed in one transaction precisely so no second org can hold any part
    // of the set. A client that could write either field has defeated domain
    // verification and the uniqueness guard together.
    match /consoleDomains/{domain} {
      allow read, write: if false;
    }

    // Cross-domain console session handoff (AGL-1902). Admin SDK only, and
    // this one is not a judgement call: a document here holds the SHA-256 of
    // two secrets that together buy a full session on somebody's account, plus
    // the uid the session would be minted for.
    //
    // A client that could READ one could compare a candidate against the
    // stored digest offline. A client that could WRITE one could set
    // `uid` to any account and `status` back to `authorized`, which is
    // account takeover with no password involved. Nothing client-side consults
    // this collection: the three legs of the flow are all server routes.
    match /authHandoffs/{requestId} {
      allow read, write: if false;
    }

    // Host → org resolver mirror, server-written beside host create.
    match /hostIndex/{hostId} {
      allow read: if isSignedIn();
      allow write: if false;
    }

    // Customer API keys (AGL-616). Secret material: only a SHA-256 hash of
    // the token is stored (doc id = hash). Deny ALL client access — minting,
    // listing (metadata only), verification, and revocation happen through
    // the Admin SDK. Clients never read this collection.
    match /apiKeys/{keyHash} {
      allow read, write: if false;
    }

    // REST API idempotency records (AGL-618): dedupe POSTs by Idempotency-Key.
    // Written and read by the Admin SDK only.
    match /apiIdempotency/{keyHash} {
      allow read, write: if false;
    }

    // Durable rate-limit counters (AGL-794): one doc per (hashed key, window),
    // written by the Admin SDK inside a transaction. Deny ALL client access —
    // a client that could write these could reset its own counter, which
    // defeats the whole point, and one that could read them could enumerate
    // which buckets are near their cap.
    match /rateLimits/{bucketId} {
      allow read, write: if false;
    }

    // Platform-wide operator settings written through audited staff routes
    // (AGL-2021). Today: `taxFiling`, which holds the jurisdiction this
    // deployment files in and the registration identifiers it files under.
    //
    // Deny ALL client access, staff included, and the identifiers are why. A
    // filing credential such as a Texas Webfile number authenticates a profile
    // at the Comptroller's eSystems; a rule that let a staff browser read this
    // document would put that credential in any session holding a staff token,
    // reachable without the audited route and without the masking the console
    // applies. Every read is an Admin-SDK read behind the staff gate, and only
    // the return itself is ever handed the whole value.
    //
    // Unmatched paths deny anyway; this match exists to carry the record.
    match /platformSettings/{settingId} {
      allow read, write: if false;
    }

    // Item 3 of the sales tax return — taxable purchases, one document per
    // filing period, entered by hand from the expense records because no
    // collection here holds the figure. Deny ALL client access: a client that
    // could write one could put an arbitrary number onto a return signed
    // under penalty of perjury, and the write is `super`-only and audited on
    // /api/admin/tax-purchases precisely so that cannot happen off the record.
    //
    // Unmatched paths deny anyway; this match exists to carry the record.
    match /platformTaxablePurchases/{period} {
      allow read, write: if false;
    }

    // Scheduled-job heartbeats (AGL-1955): one doc per cron job, stamped by
    // the run itself through the Admin SDK, read by /api/health/crons to
    // notice a job that STOPPED being scheduled. Deny ALL client access — a
    // client that could write one could forge a heartbeat for a job that is
    // not running, which is precisely the silence the check exists to break;
    // a client that could read them could enumerate the platform's job
    // schedule and when each last ran.
    //
    // Unmatched paths deny anyway; this match exists to carry the record.
    // The health endpoint is PUBLIC and reports these, so what it exposes was
    // a deliberate decision: job ids, cron expressions and ages, every one of
    // which is already in the open-source repo. The documents themselves stay
    // closed because the collection is the write side.
    match /platformCronBeats/{jobId} {
      allow read, write: if false;
    }

    // Stripe customer -> org reverse index (AGL-1028), written by the webhook
    // beside `orgs/{orgId}/billing/stripe`. It exists because moving
    // `stripeCustomerId` into a subcollection broke the webhook's
    // `where('stripeCustomerId', '==', …)` lookup, and a mapping doc is an
    // O(1) get where the alternative is a collection-group index.
    //
    // Deny ALL client access. Readable, it would map a Stripe customer id back
    // to an org for anyone who obtained one — the exact correlation this issue
    // moved the field to prevent.
    match /stripeCustomers/{stripeCustomerId} {
      allow read, write: if false;
    }

    // Per-transaction tax/revenue records (AGL-1811), written by the billing
    // webhook on `invoice.paid` and read only by the staff tax-return route
    // through the Admin SDK. Deny ALL client access: a row carries a
    // customer's billing address beside their invoice amounts.
    //
    // Unmatched paths deny anyway; this match exists to carry the record:
    // these are TAX FILING RECORDS with a statutory retention obligation, so
    // they are deliberately EXCLUDED from the GDPR org-erasure sweep
    // (`eraseOrg` — see the note there, and the erase-org-tax-retention
    // emulator spec that pins it). Do not add `platformRevenue` to any
    // org-keyed deletion pass.
    match /platformRevenue/{invoiceId} {
      allow read, write: if false;
    }

    // Storefront commerce tax records (AGL-1904), written by the commerce
    // webhook and read only by the same staff tax-return route. Deny ALL
    // client access: a row carries a shopper's address beside the amounts,
    // and it names the merchant's org — one merchant must never be able to
    // read another's sales.
    //
    // Same retention rule as `platformRevenue` above and for the same
    // reason: these are TAX records, they are org-keyed by FIELD (exactly the
    // shape the erasure sweep eats), and they must NEVER be added to an
    // org-keyed deletion pass.
    match /storefrontTaxCollected/{stripeObjectId} {
      allow read, write: if false;
    }

    // Hosts and everything under them (content-hardening review,
    // 2026-07-07: tenant sites read through the Admin SDK, so no public
    // read). Access resolves through the org membership projection
    // `memberRoles` stamped on the host doc by the org APIs (AGL-233) —
    // one get, role-aware (admin/editor write, viewer read). The legacy
    // `admins` uid-map retired with the tenants collection (AGL-238).
    function hostMemberRole(hostId) {
      return get(/databases/$(database)/documents/hosts/$(hostId))
        .data.get('memberRoles', {}).get(request.auth.uid, null);
    }
    /**
     * Is this user a member of the host AT ALL — any role, including the
     * narrowest?
     *
     * The loosest membership test in this file: `admin`, `editor`, `author`
     * and `viewer` all pass. It answers "may this person LOOK at the site
     * they were added to", and every role we sell was added to do exactly
     * that. `memberRoles` carries no entry for a non-member, so a null role
     * is the whole test.
     *
     * It is NOT an admin gate and must never be used as one. Rules that mean
     * admin-only spell the comparison out inline —
     * `hostMemberRole(hostId) == 'admin'`, as the `pluginSettings` write
     * does — so that a rule restricting something to owners cannot be
     * satisfied by a viewer. It says nothing about writing either: mutating
     * content asks `canWriteHostContent`, making it live asks
     * `canPublishHostContent`, and the documents carrying webhook secrets and
     * shopper PII ask `canReadHostSensitive`.
     */
    function isHostMember(hostId) {
      return isSignedIn() && hostMemberRole(hostId) != null;
    }
    /**
     * May this member MUTATE content on the site? (`author` included.)
     *
     * `author` (AGL-2334) is the "edit content but not publish" role the
     * agency guide sells and the product could not express. It is a HOST role
     * because publishing is a set of client-direct Firestore writes, so THIS
     * FILE is the enforcement point — there is no route in front of it to
     * gate. An org-level permission key would have been on the wrong axis
     * entirely: rules cannot evaluate a custom role without a second
     * denormalized projection, and `memberRoles` is already read here.
     */
    function canWriteHostContent(hostId) {
      return isSignedIn() &&
        hostMemberRole(hostId) in ['admin', 'editor', 'author'];
    }
    /**
     * May this member make content LIVE? (`author` excluded — that is the
     * entire role.)
     *
     * Every publish surface asks this INSTEAD OF `canWriteHostContent`, and
     * publishing is five document shapes, not one button:
     *
     *  1. `hosts/{hostId}.screens.{screenId}` — the routing map. Registering a
     *     path is what makes a page reachable at all, so this is the one that
     *     matters most and it lives on the host doc, not the screen.
     *  2. `hosts/{hostId}/screens/{screenId}` — `slug`, `publishedAt` and the
     *     `versionId` live-version pointer.
     *  3/4. the same `versionId` pointer on `layouts/{layoutId}` and
     *     `components/{componentId}` — a component republish pushes changes to
     *     every page that uses it, so it is a publish even though no route
     *     changes.
     *  5. `collections/{colId}/entries/{entryId}` — `status`/`publishedAt`/
     *     `publishAt`, a separate publish path with its own scheduler.
     *
     * And `publishSchedule` on shapes 2-4 is a SIXTH surface hiding inside
     * them. It is a client write; the executor later flips `versionId` and
     * registers the routing entry with ADMIN-SDK privileges, subject to no
     * rule at all. A publish gate that does not freeze `publishSchedule` is
     * defeated in one write — schedule it for a minute from now and the cron
     * publishes it for you. Every freeze below therefore names
     * `publishSchedule` beside `versionId`; they are one control.
     *
     * Deletion of a screen, layout or component asks this too. Not because
     * deleting is publishing, but because taking a live page down is the same
     * act in the other direction — and because the console's screen delete is
     * a soft-delete PAIRED with a routing-map removal. Letting an author do
     * half of that pair leaves a deleted screen still routed, which is a worse
     * outcome than refusing it.
     */
    function canPublishHostContent(hostId) {
      return isSignedIn() && hostMemberRole(hostId) in ['admin', 'editor'];
    }
    /**
     * May this member read the site's SENSITIVE documents — the `webhooks`
     * that carry an HMAC signing secret and a target URL, and the `orders`
     * that carry a shopper's address and payment data?
     *
     * Same membership test as `canPublishHostContent` and deliberately a
     * SEPARATE function, because it is a different question with a different
     * reason. Both read gates were `canWriteHostContent`, and widening that
     * to admit `author` would have handed every customer's PII and every
     * webhook secret to the narrowest role we sell — a role an agency grants
     * precisely BECAUSE it wants to hand a client less. "Edit content but not
     * publish" must not quietly also mean "read the order book".
     *
     * Keeping the two apart means a later decision to let authors publish
     * cannot drag the PII gate along with it by accident.
     */
    function canReadHostSensitive(hostId) {
      return isSignedIn() && hostMemberRole(hostId) in ['admin', 'editor'];
    }
    /**
     * Is the `suspended*` family on this document an ACTIVE suspension right
     * now? (AGL-1981.)
     *
     * The one definition of "suspended" both scopes ask, so the org arm
     * (AGL-210/238) and the host arm (AGL-1965) cannot drift into saying
     * different things about the same field family — which is exactly why
     * AGL-1965 declined to make its arm expiry-aware on its own.
     *
     * `suspendedUntilMs` is an optional expiry, documented as one on both
     * `organization.types.ts` and `platform.types.ts`, and honoured
     * server-side by `isLockdownActive()` since AGL-1512: past it, the tenant
     * middleware stops rewriting to `/api/locked` and the 38
     * `lockdownRefusal()` sites stop refusing. Rules read only `suspendedAt`,
     * so a TIMED suspension came back to life everywhere EXCEPT the client
     * SDK, and publishing is a client write — the customer got their site
     * back and could not edit it, with no error that said why. Since AGL-1983
     * this is also the put-back mechanism: a §512(g) counter-notice restores
     * access by stamping this field, so an expiry that never fires makes the
     * restoration clock theatre.
     *
     * Two directions of caution, both deliberate:
     *
     *  - **No expiry means indefinite**, not expired. Written as "NOT (a
     *    numeric expiry that has passed)" rather than "the expiry is in the
     *    future" precisely so an ABSENT field cannot read as a lapsed one.
     *    The tempting `data.get('suspendedUntilMs', 0) > request.time` form is
     *    that bug, and it would lift every open-ended takedown on the
     *    platform.
     *  - **A malformed expiry keeps the lock.** The field is Admin-SDK-only,
     *    so a non-number can only arrive through our own bug; `is number`
     *    makes that case leave the suspension standing. Failing closed is the
     *    right way round to be wrong here.
     *
     * The bypass this opens if the deny-lists ever slip is worth naming: once
     * rules read `suspendedUntilMs`, WRITING it is lifting the suspension. It
     * is frozen against clients on the org doc and the host doc alike
     * (AGL-1507/1517), and the rules suite re-proves that against the timed
     * fixture rather than trusting the list.
     */
    function suspensionActive(carrier) {
      return lockWindowActive(
        carrier.get('suspendedAt', null) != null,
        carrier.get('suspendedUntilMs', null));
    }
    /**
     * "A lock was placed, and its optional expiry has not passed." The ONE
     * notion of active in this file (AGL-1881), extracted from
     * `suspensionActive` above so the platform lever below shares it rather
     * than restating it. A second copy is how two panic levers end up
     * disagreeing about a window that closed one millisecond ago.
     *
     * `placed` is a boolean because the two carriers say it differently: the
     * org/host family says it with `suspendedAt`, and `lockdowns/platform`
     * says it by EXISTING — the doc's only writer is the audited staff route,
     * so a document that is there at all means someone pulled the lever
     * (`lockdownRefusedSignup()` in `libs/aglyn/.../lockdown.ts` takes the
     * identical existence-is-the-lever position, deliberately stricter than
     * `normalizeLockdownDoc`).
     *
     * Both directions of caution from AGL-1981 carry over unchanged, because
     * this IS that code: an ABSENT expiry is indefinite, never lapsed; a
     * MALFORMED one leaves the lock standing.
     */
    function lockWindowActive(placed, untilMs) {
      return placed &&
        !(untilMs is number && untilMs <= request.time.toMillis());
    }
    /**
     * THE PLATFORM PANIC BUTTON, in rules (AGL-1881).
     *
     * `/api/admin/lockdown` at platform scope writes `lockdowns/platform`,
     * and until this every enforcement point that read it was server-side:
     * the tenant middleware (pages 503), the ~36 `lockdownRefusal()` routes
     * (423), the session mint. The client SDK read it nowhere. So the panic
     * button stopped everything EXCEPT a browser tab already holding a
     * Firestore connection — and publishing is exactly that: `screen-
     * publishing.ts` writes `hosts/{h}/screens/{s}` direct from the page.
     * During the compromise this lever exists for, the attacker's open tab
     * kept writing indefinitely; not for one token lifetime, forever.
     *
     * COST, measured rather than assumed, because it is charged on every
     * non-staff host write. Padding `hostWritesFrozen` with dummy `exists()`
     * calls against the emulator until a legitimate editor write flips to
     * DENIED: the screen-update path took 3 document accesses before this
     * and takes 4 after, against the ceiling of 10 for a single-document
     * request — 6 to spare. So `exists()` and `get()` on the SAME path
     * inside one evaluation are one access, not two, and this lever is one
     * read per write, not two.
     *
     * It is the LAST disjunct of `hostWritesFrozen` on purpose: the host arm
     * reads a document three other helpers have already read (free), the org
     * arm is the next cheapest, and this one is the only one that can add a
     * new access — so a write that is refused for a narrower reason never
     * pays for it.
     *
     * The un-panic invariant (AGL-1501) is untouched and is not maintained
     * here: `isStaff()` is the first disjunct of every rule that calls
     * `hostWritesFrozen`, so the staff path short-circuits before this
     * function is reached and staff never depend on this read succeeding.
     * That ordering is the invariant — do not move this check above it.
     */
    function platformLockdownActive() {
      return exists(/databases/$(database)/documents/lockdowns/platform) &&
        lockWindowActive(
          true,
          get(/databases/$(database)/documents/lockdowns/platform)
            .data.get('untilMs', null));
    }
    // Suspension write-block (AGL-210/238): a staff-suspended org keeps
    // read access but cannot mutate content. Fail-open when the org doc
    // is missing (pre-org hosts) so legacy data never locks up.
    function orgSuspendedById(orgId) {
      return orgId != null &&
        exists(/databases/$(database)/documents/orgs/$(orgId)) &&
        suspensionActive(
          get(/databases/$(database)/documents/orgs/$(orgId)).data);
    }
    function hostOrgSuspended(hostId) {
      return orgSuspendedById(
        get(/databases/$(database)/documents/hosts/$(hostId))
          .data.get('orgId', null));
    }
    /**
     * HOST-scope lockdown (AGL-1965). The org arm above has existed since
     * AGL-210/238; this one was never written, so a host-scope takedown was a
     * takedown of everything EXCEPT the client SDK.
     *
     * `/admin/lockdown` at host scope stamps `hosts/{hostId}.suspendedAt` and
     * that stopped two of the three write paths: the public site (the tenant
     * middleware rewrites to `/api/locked`) and every Admin-SDK route (38
     * `lockdownRefusal()` sites). Publishing is neither — it is a direct
     * browser Firestore write (`screen-publishing.ts` sets
     * `hosts/{hostId}/screens/{screenId}` and the host doc's `screens` map),
     * and `screens` is not in the frozen-key list on the host update below. So
     * an admin/editor with a live session could keep editing a phishing site
     * staff had just suspended, and republish it.
     *
     * Free of charge: `hostMemberRole` and `hostOrgSuspended` already `get()`
     * this same document, and identical get()s inside one evaluation are
     * billed once. Ordered first in `hostWritesFrozen` so the cheap arm
     * short-circuits before the org arm's second get.
     *
     * Mirrors `orgSuspendedById` deliberately, and now shares its definition
     * of "suspended" outright: AGL-1981 moved the `suspendedAt`-plus-expiry
     * test into `suspensionActive()` and pointed both arms at it, so the blind
     * spot this comment used to record — neither arm consulting
     * `suspendedUntilMs`, leaving a timed suspension frozen in rules forever
     * after the server considered it served — is closed at both scopes in one
     * move, which is the only way it could be closed without the file saying
     * two different things about one field.
     */
    function hostSuspended(hostId) {
      return suspensionActive(
        get(/databases/$(database)/documents/hosts/$(hostId)).data);
    }
    /**
     * The single predicate every host write rule below asks. All THREE
     * scopes that can freeze a site freeze content writes — host, its org,
     * and (since AGL-1881) the platform; `suspendedMode: 'read-only'` (AGL-1511) is a
     * suspension too — the site keeps SERVING and stops accepting writes,
     * which is exactly this.
     *
