                     'cname', 'cnameAttachmentPending',
                     'cnameDetachmentPending', 'themeInstalledFrom',
                     'suspendedAt', 'suspendedReasonCode', 'suspendedMessage',
                     'suspendedUntilMs', 'suspendedMode', 'suspendedEnforcement',
                     'bandwidthCeiling',
                     'projectId', 'projectNumber']) &&
          // The approved-host lists join the same admin tier (AGL-1152), and
          // for the sharper of the two reasons its siblings are here: they are
          // the source the tenant's enforced CSP is BUILT from. An editor able
          // to write one could add any host on the internet to their site's
          // content-security policy — which is not "widening their own plugin
          // set", it is disabling an egress control on a page their visitors
          // load. Owner/admin only, like every other key on this document that
          // decides what a visitor's browser is allowed to do.
          //
          // ⚠️ ALL SIX, not just images. `approvedImageHosts` was alone here
          // while it was the only enforced list; the four that followed it and
          // the two added with `connect-src`/`frame-src` were writable by any
          // editor, so the tier protected the least dangerous of the set and
          // left the rest open. The two newest are the ones that would have
          // hurt most: `approvedConnectHosts` decides where a page may send
          // what a visitor typed, and `approvedFrameHosts` decides whose login
          // form it may embed.
          //
          // `authScreens` (AGL-553) belongs in this tier for the reason
          // `enabledPlugins` is in it, one step further along. That key
          // decides whether `/signin`, `/signup` and `/recover` EXIST; this
          // one decides what they RENDER. A site member who can write it
          // points the address a site's members type their password into at
          // any screen they like — the credential-confusion hazard the
          // capability exists to close, reached through the slot instead of
          // the switch.
          //
          // It is also the one live-content surface that does not pass
          // through `screens`. The tenant loader resolves the slot straight
          // to a screen document and `screenClaimsToBeAPage` asks only
          // whether it is deleted, so freezing the routing map for an
          // `author` never reached it: the role sold as "edit content but
          // not publish" could put a screen on the site's sign-in address
          // while publishing nothing. The console card writes this key from
          // the Admin -> Plugins page, which is already admin-only, so the
          // tier matches the surface that sets it.
          (hostMemberRole(hostId) == 'admin' ||
            !request.resource.data.diff(resource.data).affectedKeys()
              .hasAny(['disabledPlugins', 'enabledPlugins', 'authScreens',
                       'approvedImageHosts', 'approvedMediaHosts',
                       'approvedFontHosts', 'approvedFormActions',
                       'approvedConnectHosts', 'approvedFrameHosts'])) &&
